      "source_text": "We observe that <MATH_0001> is a profinite\ntopological group, see Fundamental Groups, Definition\n<REF_0001>.\nThe left hand categories are defined in\nSection <REF_0002>.\nThe notation used in the right hand categories is taken from\nFundamental Groups, Definition <REF_0003>\nfor sets and\nDefinition <REF_0004> for abelian groups.\nThis explains the notation.\n\n<STRUCT_0001><STRUCT_0002>\nAssertion (1) follows from\nLemma <REF_0005>\nand Fundamental Groups, Theorem <REF_0006>.\nParts (2) and (3) follow immediately from this by endowing the underlying\n(sheaves of) sets with additional structure. For example, a finite\nlocally constant sheaf of abelian groups on <MATH_0002> is the same thing\nas a finite locally constant sheaf of sets <MATH_0003>\ntogether with a map <MATH_0004>\nsatisfying the usual axioms. The equivalence in (1) sends products\nto products and hence sends <MATH_0005> to an addition on the corresponding\nfinite <MATH_0006>-set. Since <MATH_0007>-modules\nare the same thing as <MATH_0008>-sets with a compatible\nabelian group structure we obtain (2). Part (3) is proved in\nexactly the same way.\n",
      "source_text_hash": "sha256:e3967556741b4be25dbb61b34daba7b3c9ec0fd54d9cf4cc55d97f6106aa4d20",
      "unit_id": "tag:0DV5:proof"
    }
  ],
